use @piton/belay

from ./lib/CruxUiBase import CruxUiBase

export skill CruxUiStyles extends CruxUiBase:
    description:
        Builds and grows a library of very different visual styles for
        an application, such as a clean dashboard, a SaaS product or a
        technical tool. Three short questions set the directions, then
        each style is saved as a numbered sample page, shown on one
        gallery page, and described in the Piton specification so it
        can guide the look of the application.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to explore visual styles, look and feel, themes
        or design directions, wants to see very different styles side
        by side, wants to add more styles to the project's style
        library, or wants to choose a visual direction for the
        application
    prompt:
        Your goal is to grow the project's style library. A style is
        one complete visual direction for the whole application, such
        as a clean dashboard, a SaaS product, or a technical tool. Each
        run asks three questions, adds a small batch of new styles,
        shows them, and records them in the specification. The library
        keeps growing over many runs, and the specification uses it to
        decide the look of the application. Never write application
        code.

        First find the project and its library. Read the specification
        for what the application is, who uses it, its main screens and
        the domain data they show, and any design rules it already
        has, such as palette, type, spacing, icons, tone,
        accessibility and language rules. Then look for an existing
        style library anchor and its folder. Note which styles exist,
        which the user favoured or rejected, and why. If there is no
        library yet, this run creates it.

    library:
        The library has one anchor in the specification and one folder
        of pages beside it. Put the anchor where the project's
        conventions say design rules belong, and name it after what it
        holds, such as VisualStyles. If the project has no convention,
        use a design folder under the source root, such as
        spec/design/VisualStyles.pi. The folder sits next to the anchor
        file and is named after it with a .styles suffix, such as
        spec/design/VisualStyles.styles.

        Each style is one page in that folder, named by its number,
        such as 1.html, 2.html and 7.html. Numbers are global and
        permanent. A new style takes the next free number. Never
        renumber, overwrite or delete a style, even a rejected one,
        because the specification and later runs refer to styles by
        number. The folder also holds index.html, the gallery of the
        whole library.

    questions:
        Ask exactly three questions, one per turn, and show progress
        such as Question 2 of 3. Never ask what the project or an
        earlier answer already settles. When the project settles one of
        the three, use that turn for the most useful open question
        about the styles instead. Clarifications and examples about the
        same question do not use up another one. A question may offer
        options and a recommendation.

        Question 1 is which directions to add. Offer a short list of
        named directions that the library does not cover yet, such as
        clean dashboard, SaaS product, technical, editorial, playful,
        or dense enterprise, and recommend three that fit the
        application. Search the style catalogue for directions that
        suit the application and that the library lacks, so the list
        goes beyond the obvious ones. The user may pick from the list, name their own,
        or ask you to choose. Make one style per direction agreed, with
        3 as the default and 6 as the most in one run.

        Question 2 is what to lean toward and away from. Ask for
        products, sites or moods the user likes or dislikes, and for
        hard limits, such as light or dark, brand colours, contrast,
        density, or a second language.

        Question 3 is what each style shows. Recommend one
        representative screen of the application, chosen from the
        specification, such as the main list or the dashboard home, and
        ask whether that screen fits or another would show the styles
        better. Every style in a batch shows the same screen with the
        same content, so the styles can be compared fairly.

    stylePages:
        Each style page shows two things. First, the chosen screen of
        the application drawn fully in that style. Second, below it, a
        short style sheet: the colour palette as named swatches with
        their values, the type scale with the font stacks, spacing and
        corner radius, and a few basic controls, such as a button, a
        text field, a table row or a card, in their normal and focus
        states. Use system font stacks or fonts named by the project,
        never web fonts, since the page loads nothing from the network.

        Make the styles very different from each other and from every
        style already in the library. Vary what changes the whole feel,
        such as palette, light or dark base, type, density, layout
        rhythm, corners, borders, shadows, icon treatment, and tone of
        the labels. Never make styles that differ only in accent colour
        or font size. If a new style would come out close to an
        existing one, change it or say so and suggest another
        direction. Before drawing a style, read the few catalogue
        entries closest to its direction for exact, tested values and
        ideas that suit it, then make the style the project's own. Every style still meets basic accessibility: text
        has enough contrast, controls have names, and meaning never
        depends on colour alone.

        Give each style a short name and one sentence on the idea
        behind it, such as Clean dashboard: calm white panels that put
        the numbers first.

    styleGallery:
        The gallery, index.html in the library folder, shows the whole
        library. It shows one card per style with the number large and
        clear, the short name, the one-sentence idea, the user's
        verdict when there is one, and a live preview of the style page
        in an iframe. It groups styles by the run that made them,
        newest first, so the latest batch is at the top. It has a
        filter to show all, favoured, unrated or rejected styles.
        Typing a style's number and pressing Enter opens its large
        view.

    recording:
        After saving a batch and its gallery, record it in the
        specification before showing it. The library anchor holds a
        property that names the gallery path, and one property per
        style. Each style property gives its number, name, idea, page
        path and verdict, and then describes the style in plain words
        and exact values: palette with colour values and what each
        colour is for, type with font stacks and sizes, spacing,
        density, corners, borders, shadows, icons, and tone of labels.
        A builder who never opens the page must be able to apply the
        style from this description alone.

        The anchor also holds a direction property. It says which
        styles the user favours, which they rejected and why, and what
        the favoured styles have in common. Keep it as an open question
        until the user chooses. Never choose a direction for the user.
        When the user does choose, write the direction as plain design
        rules the rest of the specification can follow, and note which
        style numbers it came from. Write likes, dislikes and limits
        from question 2 here too, so later runs respect them.

    reviewing:
        Show the gallery, then ask the user what they think of the new
        styles by number. Say they can favour or reject any number,
        give a reason, ask for changes to one, mix two, or choose one
        as the direction. A change or a mix becomes a new style with
        the next free number, built from the styles it names, and the
        old styles stay as they were. Never treat silence as a verdict.

        After each answer, update the verdicts in the gallery and the
        anchor, validate, and say in one or two lines what changed.
        Then offer three options. Done ends the run. More styles starts
        a new run with three new questions. Choose a direction records
        the direction as design rules. Do not move on to designing one
        part of the UI or building the application on your own.

    resuming:
        Keep enough state to resume without storing the conversation.
        The library anchor holds the styles, their verdicts and the
        direction. The folder holds every page. On a new run, read both
        before asking anything. Never repeat a direction the library
        already has unless the user asks for a new take on it. If a
        batch has pages but no verdicts, show the gallery again and ask
        about it before starting a new batch.

    boundaries:
        This skill decides the overall look of the application and
        records it. When the user wants to design how one screen,
        component or flow works, recommend crux-ui-develop, which
        follows the chosen direction. When the work turns into a
        feature that is not about the look, recommend
        crux-spec-develop. When the user wants to apply a style to the
        application code, say that this skill stops at the
        specification and its style pages.
